# Round-4 Re-confirmation — `dao.space.index.as-observer.md`

HEAD `3d32eb4`; my r3 approval was on `0bd7550`/`e101932`; the astra round (A1–A11) is folded in at `3d32eb4`. The note grew from ~660 to 748 lines; diff reviewed in full, every new mechanism re-verified against source (including two sources I had not read before: `dao.space.transact`, the program-medium append paths). Working tree clean.

## Item 1 — §2.2 step 0, outer batch grammar: **holds**

What real media append, from code: the transactor appends **one record map per call** — `append-packet!` builds `{:dao.space/transaction {:t t :datoms datoms}}` and makes exactly one `stream/append!` (`transactor.cljc:144-155`). A program medium appends **a vector of d5 rows**: `yin.repl.core` `eval-datoms` does `(stream/append! (:program-stream state') (vec datoms))` (`yin/repl/core.cljc:427`), `test_utils/queue-ast!` does `(stream/append! … (vec (vm/ast->datoms ast)))` (`test/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc:70-74`), and `ast->datoms` rows are `[e a v t m]` vectors (`yin/vm.cljc:396-398`). Classification: record map → rule (i) ✓; bare row → first slot an integer **of either sign** (the grammar is sign-blind; `:unresolved` media's negative-`e` rows are rows — sign is admission's job, step 1) + second a keyword → rule (ii) ✓; vector of rows → first slot is a vector → rule (iii) ✓. A five-row batch's first slot is a vector, never an integer, so it **cannot** be misclassified as a row; conversely a 5-scalar vector with an int+keyword head is read as one row, which is the only coherent reading since batches-of-scalars are no medium's shape. Non-sequential non-records (entity maps, sets, scalars) fall to the whole-batch defect at position `nil` ✓. The grammar is total, deterministic, and matches every append site in the tree.

## Item 2 — §2.2 step 2 / §3.1 matrix, `m` as an identity slot: **holds**

`dao.space.transact/prepare-tx` resolves all three slots: `collect-tempids` gathers tempids from `e`, declared-ref `v`, **and `m`** — `(tempid? m-raw)` for ops (`transact.cljc:142`), `(tempid? (nth d 4))` for extra datoms (`:150`) — and `apply-tempid-map` rewrites `m-raw` (`:179-181`), matching the astra citation. What "reserved" contains, from `datom.cljc`: `{0 :db/retract, 1 :db/assert, 2 :db/derived}` (`:14-19`), ids 0–15 reserved, `first-user-id` 16 (`:27-39`). Real `m` values on a transactor medium: the markers and metadata-entity ids allocated from `first-user-id` up (`transact.cljc:206` base-eid floor, `:216-236` alloc-base, `expand-m-map`'s `:128` m ← the op's own allocated eid) — never anything else, because the transactor's `local-datom?` validation rejects non-integer `m`. The matrix's `m` row (`:resolved`: reserved or user-positive, tempid → defect; `:unresolved`: reserved passes, tempid → allocated, user-positive → defect) is exactly consistent with those values, and the `:unresolved` column is what fixes A1 (a macro log's `m = ev`, a negative event tempid). One non-issue checked: `transact`'s `tempid?` also accepts `"tid_"` strings (`:10-12`), but those never reach a stream — `prepare-tx` resolves them to integers before the transactor validates — so the index's negative-integer-only tempids are consistent.

## Item 3 — §4.1 publication state machine `{:payloads :next}`: **holds**

Traced against `observer.cljc:183-208`: **ok/full/full/ok** — `flush-staged` appends from `:next`, advancing after each `ok`; `full` on payload *i* leaves `:next = i` and not-ready, and the next round resumes at *i* through the loop's not-ready path (`run` before any read, `:184-188`) — an accepted payload is never re-appended by an ordinary retry, and blobs accepted before a `full` on the manifest are not re-sent (the Phase 0′ "refusal at the manifest" test). **Terminal mid-list** — throws with the staged state, `:next` included; since the throw happens in `run` at round entry, the caller's retained session already carries the same `:next`, and the ex-data belt-and-braces covers callers that don't retain it. **Resumption** — repair, resume at `:next`, done. **Drain only at the end** — the handle's content is drained only when `:next` reaches the end (manifest accepted); mid-staging the `:payloads` list itself holds the blobs, so the handle is never needed for re-derivation. Manifest-last keeps every partial prefix a non-publication. The A4 defect trace confirms the Phase 0′ gate is the right dependency: a round that *completes* a staged publication then throws at a later `load` would, without the partial-session fix, hand back the pre-round session with `:staged` still set → retry re-appends the whole publication; with `fold-batch` total, a load-throw is only a fold bug, and the §5 fix (post-run consumer carried in `ex-data`) is both necessary and sufficient. The at-most-once-per-staged-occurrence vs `jing/materialize!`-dedup distinction is stated honestly.

## Item 4 — §4.2 checkpoint promotion: **holds**

Implementable exactly with what exists: `read-manifest` (present + shape + content-address match, `index.cljc:269-285`) plus `bt/walk-addresses` over each of the four index roots (`btree.cljc:2101-2118` — every reachable address reported root-first; nil root walks to nothing, `:2117`), with `on-address` a presence check via `jing/get` (`jing.cljc:301-307`, not-found on absence); a missing *branch* blob additionally throws from `KVStorage.-restore` (`storage.cljc:48-50`). It correctly detects an unmaterialized publication: the check reads durable state at promotion time, so a manifest (or blob) still sitting in an in-memory intake is simply absent → not promoted → the previous verified checkpoint stands, and recovery from a never-recoverable latest publication is never a special case. The check is state-based and conservative — no race with the async observer beyond the check's own read.

## Item 5 — §5 `index/db-value`: **holds**

The `:dao.space.query/published` key is only ever tested with `contains?` — `value?` (`query.cljc:150`), `realize-db-value!` (`:386`), `rows` (`:512`) — its value is never dereferenced, so an in-process value `{:dao.space.query/published <marker> :indexes <live trees> :rows (delay …)}` is recognised by the existing path unchanged: the `:published` branch realizes via `covered-indexes` (structural — `index.cljc:345-359` already documents that an in-memory index map is indistinguishable and fine) plus `:rows` through `force-relation` (`query.cljc:337-343`), and `current` with no `as-of` routes through the covered sets (`:359-361`). No `query` code changes; the new constructor lives in `index`, which requires nothing from `query` (`index.cljc:35-39`) — "query gains one source kind" happens by construction, and the layering claim (`index` below `query`) is true. Even `close-published!` on such a value is harmless (`jing/close!` is nil-safe, `jing.cljc:318-325`). The "live means folded-up-to-the-cursor" definition and the Phase 1 rewording ("the test drives the indexer, then queries") remove A5's observation-boundary ambiguity.

## Item 6 — `:max-t`: **holds, with one unspecified edge (→ R4-2)**

`derive-next-t`: `0` for empty history, else `1 + max t` over all retained datoms (`transactor.cljc:117-131`). For every nonempty case, `1 + max(max-t at checkpoint, max t of folded suffix)` — §4.2's "max-t over checkpoint plus folded suffix, plus one" — matches exactly, and the three restart cases are correctly distinguished (the in-process writer-task reopen is `dao.space.md` §Fault Tolerance's "a writer task stops; the process lives"; the memory-log case degrades to today's behavior, which for an empty new log is `0`). The gap: the note never states `:max-t`'s **initial value**. With the natural `0`-init, an empty checkpoint yields watermark `1` where `derive-next-t` answers `0`; the formula is only correct if `:max-t` starts at `−1` ("no writer t folded yet"). One clause fixes it.

## Item 7 — previously verified claims after the rewrite: **hold, with one new tension (→ R4-1)**

Re-checked on the current text: admission (§2.2 step 1, unchanged in substance from r2/r3 — `local-datom?` minus the `e ≥ 0` clause, `v` unconstrained, records per-datom + shape checks); `conj`/dirty-tracking (§2.2 step 3 unchanged — persistent insert, dirtiness = absence of an address mark, `node-store`'s per-slot skip, `btree.cljc:1059-1064`); `:strong` per session kind (§4.1 unchanged from r3 — settings from the tree, resumed sessions restore through a session-constructed `:strong` kv-storage, not the read path; re-verified against `make-ref`/`make-store-ref`/`resident-root`); `current`/`history` (§2.3 — the new history caveat is accurate: over a `db-value`, history's order is the EAVT walk of the `:rows` delay (`index.cljc:288-294`, "in index order"), not `t` order; the `as-of` two-clocks warning is correct, `bound-datoms` filters `t` numerically, `query.cljc:68-73`). The retraction paragraph's "the same *resolved* `e`" qualifier is right (tempid reuse across batches is a different entity — resolution facts keep them disjoint). A9's logical-equality test premise is sound from the code: `from-sequential`'s `split-ranges` bulk fill (`btree.cljc:1993-2051`) and incremental `conj` splits partition the same rows differently, so manifest inequality is expected and rows/counts/query equality is the correct acceptance test.

The specific item-7 question: the matrix's `:resolved` column for `e` ("reserved → defect") does **not** reject anything the transactor writes *within contract* — `datom.md` reserves 0–15 and user entities start at `first-user-id`. But it **does** reject `e ∈ [0,16)`, which `local-datom?` admits (`datom.cljc:51-52` checks non-negative only), which the transactor will write if a caller picks such a `:db/id` (`transactor.cljc:56-90` validates via `local-datom?` only), and which `snapshot-datoms`/`index-datoms`/`publish-index!` all fold today (`index.cljc:378-384`). So a log carrying a reserved-`e` row defects under the session while folding under the one-shot path — Phase 0′'s parity claim silently depends on test data avoiding `e ∈ [0,16)`. See R4-1.

## Findings

**[P3 — must address before Phase 0′ test data is chosen] R4-1. Reserved-`e` on `:resolved`: the session is stricter than every existing path, and the parity claim depends on it silently.** Either pass reserved `e` through on `:resolved` (matching `local-datom?`/`element-datoms`, making parity unconditional), or state explicitly that reserved-`e` rows defect, that this is a deliberate enforcement of `datom.md`'s reservation that the runtime never enforced, and that Phase 0′ parity holds only for logs whose ids respect `first-user-id`. Loud either way (defects, not silent merges), so P3 — but it should be a written decision, not an emergent test failure.

**[P3] R4-2. `:max-t`'s initial value is unspecified; `0`-init breaks the empty-checkpoint watermark.** `derive-next-t` answers `0` for empty history (`transactor.cljc:123-124`); "max-t + 1" then requires `:max-t = −1` when no writer `t` has been folded. State the initial.

**[P3] R4-3. The candidate's `:cursor` has no supplier in the listed signatures.** §2.1 lists `publish! : index-state → index-state'` and `checkpoint : index-state → candidate`, but the cursor lives in the observer half of the session, which the index state never sees; §4.2 says "capture *is* the publish's return value". One sentence: `publish!`/`checkpoint` takes the session (or the observer's cursor), or the composition pairs the return with `(:cursor observer)` at the same boundary — and the nothing-folded-in-between rule then binds whoever does the pairing.

**[P3] R4-4. The mode matrix's category definitions overlap as written.** "'Reserved' is any id below `first-user-id` … 'tempid' is any negative integer" — a negative id is both; the cells only parse as the partition reserved `[0,16)` / user-positive `[16,∞)` / tempid `(−∞,0)`. Say the partition. (Same wording batch: §2.3's "its implementation preserves source order, EAVT over a tree" is compressed past unambiguity — over a `db-value` history is EAVT-walk order, not source order; the *promise* ("no ordering; order by `t` explicitly") is right and is what matters.)

No P1s or P2s. A1–A11's resolutions are all real mechanisms verified against code: A1 (item 2), A2 (the `:yin/source-call` split is the correct fix for a real double-duty attribute — `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 writes it as both an external coordinate and a log-local ref while `event-schema` declares it a ref — and gating Phase 2 on the amendment is the right ordering), A3 (item 4), A4 (item 3), A5 (item 5), A6 (matrix, with R4-1's nuance), A7 (item 6), A8 (item 1), A9 (sound premise), A10/A11 (wording, checked).

## Verdict

**APPROVE.** All seven verification items hold against the code (items 6 and 7 with edge gaps that are wording, not mechanism), the astra round's three P1s and six P2s are resolved by mechanisms that exist or are implementable exactly as specified, my previously verified claims survive the rewrite, and the four new findings are P3 spec-tightenings — R4-1 to be settled in a sentence before Phase 0′'s parity test data is chosen, the rest one clause each. The note is now materially stronger than the text I approved at `e101932`, and nothing in the changes since contradicts the code.
